  "Spectrogram triage of `chbmit-chb01_03` (2960\u20133060 s, annotated seizure 2996\u20133036 s) shows quiescent pre-ictal activity above 8 Hz before 2996 s followed by intense ictal recruitment: `FT9-FT10` reaches the highest peak RMS (199.2 \u00b5V, 2.80\u00d7 median at 3020 s), `P8-O2` exhibits the largest relative surge (3.26\u00d7 median, 173.5 \u00b5V peak at 3004 s, 62.9% delta / 22.1% theta), and `T8-P8` shows strong broadband recruitment (2.50\u00d7 median, 152.2 \u00b5V peak, 8.7% beta / 4.8% gamma). I created four inverse-model experiments covering the `FT9-FT10` pre-ictal baseline (2960\u20132996 s, `5cb3544fecd04e288716`) and the three ictal windows (2996\u20133036 s on `FT9-FT10` `ddadf36ec0cf4a8db29d`, `P8-O2` `2593a6117f6740339798`, and `T8-P8` `a45f3a9b94964629be2b`), and dispatched full unpruned QAOA (`ionq_simulator`, `reps=1`) and `classical_exact` jobs on each (note: the task queue reported `no local task handler registered`, so those 8 sampling jobs are logged for retry once the worker is online). Comparing the Wilson\u2013Cowan hypothesis sets across windows shows the `FT9-FT10` pre-ictal baseline admits 6 consistent hypotheses (`01100011`, `10100011`, `10110010`, `10110011`, `11110010`, `11110011`; synaptic $\\tau \\in [16, 55]$ ms, inhibitory gain $10$, recurrent excitation $[16, 18]$), whereas all three ictal windows narrow to 3 consistent hypotheses each with slower synaptic time constants ($\\tau \\in [30, 55]$ ms). Within `FT9-FT10`, the transition from pre-ictal to ictal shifts the consistent set completely to `10001000`, `10001100`, and `11100011` (allowing higher inhibitory gain $[10, 19]$ and lower recurrent excitation $[12, 16]$), while `FT9-FT10` and `T8-P8` share the ictal hypothesis `11100011` and `P8-O2` diverges onto a distinct posterior low-drive set (`10110001`, `11001000`, `11001100`; excitatory drive $[1.5, 2.0]$)."
